{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"security_deposits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"New Jersey security deposit transfer, return and withholding penalties","requirement":"When a rental property is sold, the new owner must obtain the tenant's security deposit plus accrued interest and remains responsible for investing it, paying interest and returning it; willfully withholding a deposit from a tenant receiving government rental assistance is penalized.","key_value":"$500-$2,000 civil penalty per offense","coverage_conditions":"Residential security deposits regulated by N.J.S.A. 46:8-19 et seq.; deposit disputes are heard in the Superior Court, Special Civil Part.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"All residential rental security deposits"},"exemptions":null,"penalty":"Civil penalty of $500 to $2,000 for each offense for willfully withholding the deposit of a tenant receiving government assistance; a fine of at least $200 or up to 30 days' imprisonment for unlawfully using deposit monies.","effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 46:8-21.1","quoted_span":"Any landlord who willfully and intentionally withholds a security deposit made by or on behalf of a tenant who has received financial assistance through any Stat e or federal program, including welfare or rental assistance , may be penalized. The landlord may be liable for a civil penalty of not less than $500 or not more than $2,000 for each offense.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.7,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"New Jersey Anti-Eviction Act good cause requirement","requirement":"A residential landlord may recover possession only with the tenant's consent or through the legal eviction process, and may not evict or refuse to renew a lease without good cause that the landlord can prove in court.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"Residential dwelling units used as a residence, under written or oral leases; all residential landlords.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"Residential dwelling units used as a residence"},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.1","quoted_span":"No residential landlord may evict or fail to renew a lease, whether it is a written or an oral lease without good cause. The landlord must be able to prove in court that he has grounds for an eviction.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.7,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"rent_increase_limits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"New Jersey newly constructed multiple dwellings rent control exemption","requirement":"Newly constructed multiple dwellings are exempt from any local rent control or rent leveling ordinance for a period of 30 years following completion of construction of the building.","key_value":"30 years","coverage_conditions":"Newly constructed multiple dwellings in New Jersey; rents subsidized by government funding may also be exempt from local rent control ordinances.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":"construction_date","min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"Newly constructed multiple dwellings, exempt for 30 years after completion of construction"},"exemptions":"Newly constructed multiple dwellings within 30 years of completion; government-subsidized rents may also be exempt; separately, HUD-subsidized and NJHMFA-financed projects are not subject to municipal rent control.","penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:42-84.5","quoted_span":"Under the Newly Constructed Multiple Dwellings Law ( N.J.S.A. 2A:42 -84.5), newly constructed multiple dwellings shall be exempt from any local rent control ordinances for a period of 30 years following completion of construction of the building.","interaction":"Overrides local rent control ordinances for newly constructed multiple dwellings during the 30-year period.","confidence":0.75,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"rent_increase_limits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"New Jersey rent increase notice and unconscionability rule","requirement":"A landlord may not raise rent mid-lease: the old lease must be terminated and the tenant offered a new lease at the increased rent, with a notice to quit and notice of rent increase; the increase must not be unconscionable.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"Residential tenancies in New Jersey; increases must comply with any municipal ordinance governing rent increases.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"Residential tenancies"},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.1","quoted_span":"A landlord cannot raise the rent mid-lease term. The old lease must be terminated, and the new lease must have the increased rental payment.","interaction":"Where a municipal rent control ordinance exists, an increase must comply with it.","confidence":0.7,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"screening_restrictions","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"New Jersey Law Against Discrimination source of lawful income protection","requirement":"The Law Against Discrimination prohibits a landlord or rental agency from discriminating in the rental of housing, including on the basis of source of lawful income, and requires equal treatment in rentals.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"All landlord-tenant relationships in New Jersey.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":true,"other":"Excludes two-family owner-occupied dwellings, rooms in owner- or resident-occupied single homes, and single-sex residences as to familial status"},"exemptions":"Two-family owner-occupied dwellings, rooms in an owner- or resident-occupied single home, and residences planned for and occupied by one sex, as it pertains to familial status.","penalty":"Up to $10,000 for a first offense under the LAD; a discriminating landlord may be required to pay damages and rent the unit to the complainant.","effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 10:5-12","quoted_span":"The New Jersey Law Against Discrimination (LAD), N.J.S.A. 10:5-12(g) to -(h), prohibits discrimination when selling or renting property and requires equal treatment in the sale or rental of housing regardless of race, creed, color, national origin, ancestry, sex, marital status, civil union status, domestic partnership status, familial status, affectional or sexual orientation, gender  identity or expression, mental and physical disability, nationality, or source of lawful income.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.7,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
